# No competitor names; product name only on About (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-27):

> **NEVER mention the name of my competitors in the web site contents.
> Refer to them as "Competitors". Minimize the use of the product name
> to only the Help About screen only.**

## The rule

> **Operator-facing website copy never names a competitor brand.
> Write "Competitors" (localized). The marketed product name appears
> only on Help → About. Everywhere else, describe the function
> (this server, the console, the integrated kit) without the mark.**

## Website contents (in scope)

Admin SPA chrome, i18n catalogs, in-product Help/manuals, login,
banners, dialogs, and any other string that ships to the browser.
Includes `defaultValue` in `ui/src` and comments that ship in the
bundle.

**Not website:** `docs/dev`, Cursor rules, commit bodies, e2e that
is not imported by `ui/src`, Open Source Credits **copyright**
lines that name an upstream licensor.

## Competitors

Do not print these (or product SKUs) in website copy. The list is
for agents so they can find leaks. Website text says **Competitors**.

Forbidden examples: CyberArk, Delinea, BeyondTrust, HashiCorp,
1Password, Bitwarden, Thycotic, WALLIX, PowerBroker, Broadcom PAM,
Avecto, Keeper, Secret Server, Privilege Cloud.

Comparison copy: "application launchers used by Competitors."
A connector that would have used a competitor brand: generic
function name (external secrets vault), never the brand.

## Product name (About only)

The marketed name is `product_name` / `product_name_mark` /
`{{productName}}` (currently CMMC Complete). Show it **only** on
Help → About (`/about/version` and sibling About & Licensing
pages). Do not put it on login, the drawer, Settings, banners,
next-steps, downloads, or dashboards.

Prefer "this server", "the console", "the integrated kit".
Do not repeat the mark to "explain" honesty banners.

## Forbidden

- Competitor brands in SPA strings, Help, or manuals
- `{{productName}}` / CMMC Complete outside About
- Closing this as Met or certified

## Origin

2026-08-27 — operator hard rule.
